#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184906} :activity/id #uuid "8038dbd6-9c0f-5fe5-be97-e9a0082ee6ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185366 :tutorial/content "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x?$ \n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1})+(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed $c_1+c_2=C.$ (The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)" :tutorial/id #uuid "207bbd12-158a-58be-b000-82cace6dcdde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 701 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "61a29b78-51b2-509a-bb12-5c2009c1ee92" :step/math-academy-id 1351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184057 :question/id #uuid "e4a94ca8-82ee-5776-a46d-b735d4ab60fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-371" :question/problem "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567064}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"} :step/id #uuid "319d1f65-0963-5490-81bd-5f970237cb5a" :step/math-academy-id 1352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185367 :tutorial/content "For more complicated functions consisting of the sum of functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the constant factor and sum rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int k_1 f(x) \\pm k_2 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x = k_1\\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm k_2\\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b5ce4c9-ef44-52d9-a506-96aa15055e00" :tutorial/math-academy-id 703 :tutorial/title "Combining the Constant Factor and Sum Rules"} :step/id #uuid "22856be8-19a5-564b-abcd-7c797b0d4fa8" :step/math-academy-id 1354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184058 :question/id #uuid "ea7dadee-2b44-5e62-bef4-26fb0cb185ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-372" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2 x^3 - x \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{3}-x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605135}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum and Constant Factor Rules"} :step/id #uuid "9484fd24-4333-5bcd-94ff-4d3a59b12f87" :step/math-academy-id 1355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184059 :question/id #uuid "8f99b603-2fba-5a58-9966-b91d3a8a773e" :question/math-academy-id "e-373" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left( 5(\\sqrt{x})^{3} - 4 x^2 + 5\\right) \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5(\\sqrt{x})^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{3/2}-4{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{2}\\text{d}x+5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\frac{5}{2}}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+5(x)+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+5x+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+5x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "007f3355-427a-5da4-8af7-22581f1cb1de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567046}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sums of Power Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2dbf153e-76a0-59b0-be0c-70a7074b3f96" :step/math-academy-id 1357}] :activity/title "The Sum Rule for Indefinite Integrals" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184368}]}
